<Layout>
  <h1>an archive of <span class="proper">tora vyl drak</span></h1>

  <p class="lede">
    a sanctuary built inside a volcanic caldera, high in the bones of the cascade range, twenty-five hundred years after the sky fell — its people, its places, its language, and the long quiet history that shaped them.
  </p>

  <hr class="kahu-rule" />

  <p>
    in the year 4625 of the old calendar, the <span class="proper">kahu</span> live inside an eighteen-mile caldera in the cascade range. they call their home <span class="proper">tora vyl drak</span> — literally <em>last call hope</em>, the last call of hope for humanity. the name is earned: in the year 2075, an asteroid on collision course with earth was struck by the combined nuclear arsenals of the world's superpowers in an attempt to redirect it. the strike broke the asteroid in two but did not turn it; both halves struck, and the nuclear fallout rode down with them. what the impacts didn't destroy, the fallout poisoned. in the centuries that followed, a fungal organism spread across the wounded surface of the world — <span class="kt">peli-shen</span>, danger-life — whose airborne spores became lethal below five thousand feet of elevation. the world below the spore line became unlivable. the world above is the <span class="proper">kahu</span>: roughly two hundred and seventy-eight thousand people inside the caldera, woven into a tapestry of smaller kahu, tending each other and the systems that keep them alive.
  </p>

  <p>
    the <span class="proper">kahu</span> are not entirely alone. the <span class="proper">zou-esh</span> — the small, devoted order of scholar-warriors who survive the training — leave the sanctuary on long missions: searching for survivors, gathering supplies, and recording what the world below the rim has become. across the centuries, the survivors they have brought home are the threads from which the tapestry is woven.
  </p>

  <p>
    this is the encyclopedia of that world. it is being written, slowly, by hand. the voice here is objective — a record. stories told from inside the world live elsewhere.
  </p>

